WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.179
Stop reasons: the success cases you must still handle.

2
00:00:03.459 --> 00:00:08.216
A 200 response means the request succeeded, not that the answer is usable.

3
00:00:08.496 --> 00:00:14.277
Check stop_reason after every call and decide explicitly what each value means for your code.

4
00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:17.437
end_turn is the normal case: the model finished.

5
00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:21.429
max_tokens means the response hit your ceiling and is truncated.

6
00:00:21.709 --> 00:00:27.533
If you were expecting JSON, it is probably invalid; if you show it to a user, mark it as cut off.

7
00:00:27.813 --> 00:00:34.277
The fix is to raise max_tokens or continue the response, not to retry the identical request.

8
00:00:34.557 --> 00:00:38.013
stop_sequence means one of your custom stop strings fired.

9
00:00:38.293 --> 00:00:43.477
tool_use means the model wants you to run a tool and send back a result, which is week 3.

10
00:00:43.757 --> 00:00:51.693
pause_turn appears with server-side tools such as web search when a long tool loop pauses; you send the paused turn back to continue.

11
00:00:51.973 --> 00:00:59.525
refusal means the model declined, and it arrives as HTTP 200, so it never reaches your exception handler.

12
00:00:59.805 --> 00:01:04.285
On current models a stop_details object names the policy category.

13
00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:11.925
Anthropic’s guidance is to retry on a fallback model, which the API can do for you on several models, or to rephrase.

14
00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:21.656
Treat refusals as a product event you log and review, because a pattern of refusals on legitimate traffic is a prompt or routing problem you can fix.

15
00:01:21.936 --> 00:01:27.909
model_context_window_exceeded means the output filled the context window; treat it like truncation.
